### 逐处错误标注
1. hidden threatens → hidden threats（threaten 是动词，这里需要名词 threat）
2. if really opening it → if it is really opened（状语从句缺主语，it 指代 library）
3. Those support opening → Those supporting opening（support 改为现在分词作后置定语）
4. common sevices → public service（拼写 service；公共服务固定搭配 public service）
5. should to be → should be（情态动词 should 后直接加动词原形，去掉 to）
6. everybody have rights → everyone has the right（不定代词 everybody 作主语，谓语用单数；right 前面加 the）
7. bring students' safety troubles → bring safety troubles to students（语序问题）
8. anybody should not be allowed → outsiders should not be allowed（anybody 语义模糊，这里指校外人员 outsiders）
9. taking some limitations → setting some limits（固定搭配 set limits 设立限制，不用 take limitation）
10. everyday merely allowing → only allowing ... every day（everyday 是形容词；every day 作状语；merely 位置不对）
11. can people gain knowledge and protecting → can people gain knowledge and protect（only 倒装句，and 并列两个原形 gain、protect）

### 修正
	When it comes to opening university libraries to the public, opinions vary from person to person. Some believe that it will greatly help nearby residents gain knowledge, while
others argue that there will be hidden threats on campus if it is really opened.
	Those supporting opening claim that as a fundamental public service, university libraries should be opened to the public and everyone has the right to study by reading books.
However, others maintain that permitting strangers to visit the school may bring safety troubles to students and other uncertain problems, including environmental issues and
disruption to students. Therefore, plenty of people say that for students' safety, outsiders should not be allowed to enter the school.
	As far as I am concerned, I prefer setting some limits for visitors. For example, only allowing a certain number of people to visit every day and preventing them from 
wandering around everywhere. Only in this way can people gain knowledge and protect students at the same time.

### 三、优化完整版（四级高分版，同思路）

	When it comes to whether university libraries should be open to the public, opinions vary widely. Some hold the view that local residents can get access to rich reading resources,
while others worry that opening libraries may cause potential risks to campus.
	People in favor of the policy argue that university libraries are public resources, so they should serve more people. Residents without easy access to books can broaden their
horizons there. On the contrary, opponents point out that outsiders may disturb students’ study and even bring safety risks. Too many visitors may also damage library facilities 
and spoil the quiet study atmosphere
	From my perspective, university libraries can be open to the public with proper rules. We can set a daily visitor quota and restrict visitors to the reading area. Only in this
way can we share resources and guarantee students’ study environment at the same time.
